# ASK2PASS — Adult Education Module
## The ABC of Reading and Writing
### Architecture & Functional Specification

## 1. Purpose

The Adult Education Module — The ABC of Reading and Writing — provides a dedicated progressive literacy pathway for adult learners.

It develops:

- reading
- writing
- comprehension
- vocabulary
- communication
- practical functional literacy

The programme progresses continuously from foundational literacy toward independent literacy and mastery.

---

## 2. Programme Identity

**Module Name:** Adult Education Module — The ABC of Reading and Writing

**Acronym:** AEM

**Programme Type:** Adult Education / Functional Literacy

ABC represents the foundational nature of the programme and does not restrict the programme to alphabet instruction.

The complete curriculum uses an A–Z mastery framework.

---

## 3. Architectural Principle

AEM is not a new learning engine.

It uses the existing ASK2PASS:

- AI Engine
- Adaptive Learning
- Learning Path
- Learning Orchestration
- Learning Delivery
- Learning Runtime
- Learning Progress
- Assessment Engine
- Learner Records
- Lesson Records

The existing AI Engine remains the learning engine.

Adult Education supplies its own curriculum, progression rules and mastery policy.

AEM does not duplicate existing engines.

DSC and TCC/DCC are not used as the Adult Education progression mechanism.

---

## 4. Curriculum Model

The programme uses an A–Z master curriculum framework.

A–Z represents curriculum domains, not merely 26 lessons.

Each domain may contain dynamically generated topics, exercises, reviews and assessments.

### A — Alphabet & Print Awareness

Letter recognition, uppercase/lowercase, alphabet sequence, print awareness and letter identification.

### B — Beginning Sounds

Initial sounds, sound discrimination and letter-to-sound relationships.

### C — Consonants & Vowels

Vowels, consonants, short/long vowel awareness and core sound patterns.

### D — Decoding

Sound blending, syllables, CVC patterns and decoding unfamiliar simple words.

### E — Early Word Reading

Simple words, high-frequency words, word recognition and word-picture association.

### F — Phonics & Word Families

Common sound patterns, rhyming, word families and pattern recognition.

### G — Guided Reading

Simple sentences, reading fluency and extracting meaning from short sentences.

### H — High-Frequency Vocabulary

Common everyday words, personal information, family, places, time and objects.

### I — Information Reading

Signs, labels, notices, instructions, forms and short messages.

### J — Joining Words

Combining words, sentence formation and basic conjunctions.

### K — Key Vocabulary

Everyday vocabulary expansion, opposites, synonyms and contextual meaning.

### L — Listening to Reading

Hear → identify → read → understand → use.

### M — Meaning & Comprehension

Who, what, where, when, why, main idea and simple inference.

### N — Numbers & Numeracy Literacy

Reading numbers, dates, prices, quantities, addresses and practical numerical information.

### O — Oral-to-Written Language

Speaking-to-writing, dictation, everyday communication and expressing simple ideas.

### P — Practical Writing

Names, addresses, notes, lists, messages and basic forms.

### Q — Question Literacy

Understanding, reading and answering questions; asking questions and question words.

### R — Reading Fluency

Accuracy, repeated reading, phrase recognition and progressively longer passages.

### S — Sentence Construction

Subject, verb, object, word order, capitalization and punctuation.

### T — Text Understanding

Short paragraphs, main ideas, supporting details, sequence and simple summaries.

### U — Useful Everyday Documents

Notices, receipts, instructions, schedules and public/workplace information.

### V — Vocabulary Expansion

Context, word relationships, meaning and usage.

### W — Writing Development

Sentence writing, short paragraphs, descriptions and functional communication.

### X — Expression

Communicating ideas, written responses, descriptions and organizing thoughts.

### Y — Your Everyday Literacy

Integrated practical reading, writing, listening, understanding and communication.

### Z — Independent Literacy & Mastery

Independent reading, comprehension, writing and practical functional-literacy performance.

---

## 5. Progressive Learning Rule

AEM uses continuous progressive learning.

The system automatically selects or generates the next appropriate learning activity using:

- current mastery
- previous performance
- prerequisite mastery
- adaptive difficulty
- learner history
- assessment results

The learner normally cannot manually jump to arbitrary unmastered topics.

The backend controls the authorized progression boundary.

Progression is mastery-based, not calendar-based.

---

## 6. Mastery and Authorized Topic Jumping

A learner may request a later topic or domain of interest.

A request does not automatically unlock that topic.

The system may first generate an entry/proficiency assessment covering the required prerequisites.

### If the learner passes

The system may:

- recognize demonstrated competencies
- authorize the requested topic
- record the assessment evidence
- update progression
- preserve previous records
- continue adaptive learning from the authorized position

### If the learner does not pass

The system shall:

- keep the requested topic locked
- identify prerequisite weaknesses
- generate reinforcement
- return the learner to the appropriate progression point

Therefore, topic jumping is permitted only through demonstrated competence.

---

## 7. Daily Study Cycle

A normal AEM study cycle is **40 minutes**.

### Lesson — 25 minutes

Adaptive teaching and learning activity.

The 25 minutes is a lesson duration, not a one-topic duration.

A fast learner may complete one, two, three or more topics within the period where mastery is demonstrated.

### Review — 5 minutes

Review of learning completed during the study cycle.

### CBT Quiz — 10 minutes

A short computer-based assessment covering the study-cycle learning.

### Total

**25 + 5 + 10 = 40 minutes.**

The 40-minute cycle is not a topic boundary.

---

## 8. Break Rule

After a normal 40-minute study cycle, the system establishes a break boundary.

The break does not impose a daily study limit.

The learner may return and begin another study cycle whenever they choose.

There is no imposed limit on:

- study cycles per day
- study days
- lifetime study sessions

The learner may continue studying at any time.

---

## 9. Continuous Study

The backend preserves learning continuity across sessions.

When the learner logs in or enters the classroom, the system should restore:

- A–Z position
- current domain
- current topic
- mastery state
- recent activity
- completed topics
- reinforcement requirements
- next authorized topic
- study-cycle status

The learner should continue from the correct point without manually searching for where they stopped.

If a learner leaves during a study cycle, the appropriate session state must be retained.

---

## 10. Progression Chart

The Adult Education classroom shall display a persistent progression chart.

It must be visible when the learner enters the classroom and when the learner completes or logs out of a learning session.

Conceptually:

A → B → C → D → E → F → G → H → I → J → K → L → M
                              ↑
                           CURRENT

N → O → P → Q → R → S → T → U → V → W → X → Y → Z

The chart shall show:

- completed domains
- current domain
- current topic
- mastered topics
- reinforcement topics
- authorized jumps
- upcoming authorized learning

Each A–Z domain may contain multiple topics.

The chart therefore represents both domain-level and topic-level progress.

The backend is authoritative for progression.

---

## 11. Adaptive Learning

Adaptive mode is mandatory.

The system dynamically controls:

- topic selection
- difficulty
- explanations
- practice quantity
- reinforcement
- review
- assessment generation
- progression decisions

Fast learners may progress faster.

Learners requiring reinforcement receive additional adaptive instruction.

The system must not force equal pacing on all learners.

Adaptive learning may permit several topics to be completed within one 25-minute lesson when mastery is demonstrated.

---

## 12. Language Controls

The classroom shall provide language controls.

Language support shall integrate with the AI learning experience and may affect:

- explanations
- instructions
- examples
- spoken/audio support
- clarification
- learner interaction

Language configuration must remain extensible.

Changing language does not bypass curriculum prerequisites or mastery requirements.

---

## 13. Monthly Examination

Every AEM learner must undertake a monthly examination.

It contains:

1. CBT examination
2. Essay/writing examination

The monthly examination is recorded in the learner's formal assessment history.

It is separate from the normal 10-minute study-cycle CBT.

### Study-cycle CBT

- 10 minutes
- frequent
- formative
- measures current learning

### Monthly Examination

- formal periodic assessment
- CBT component
- Essay/writing component
- recorded in assessment history

---

## 14. Lesson Notes

A lesson note shall be filed after every completed lesson/study activity using the existing ASK2PASS lesson-record architecture.

The record should capture, where applicable:

- learner
- date/time
- programme
- A–Z domain
- topics covered
- objectives
- adaptive state
- performance
- mastery outcome
- review result
- CBT result
- next authorized topic
- reinforcement requirements

Lesson notes remain accessible through the learner's records.

AEM shall not create a duplicate lesson-record system.

---

## 15. Assessments and Records

AEM learners receive the normal ASK2PASS learning-record discipline.

Records shall include:

- lesson records
- progression records
- mastery records
- study-session records
- CBT quiz records
- monthly examination records
- essay assessment records
- adaptive learning history
- authorized progression jumps
- reinforcement history

All records are persistent and backend-authoritative.

An authorized progression jump must not erase previous learning history.

Assessment results and mastery evidence remain available for future adaptive decisions and classroom continuity.
